  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nThis afternoon...Mid and high clouds moving into San Diego County\nfrom the south could produce isolated light showers and possibly \na tstm or two over the next several hours, mainly in the mountains\nbut a few light showers could develop over the valleys and coastal\nareas. Temperatures are mostly a few degrees higher than at this \ntime yesterday.\n\nThrough friday...High pressure will continue to bring hot weather to\nSocal. High temperatures will be around 10 degrees above average \nwith low temperatures as much as 15 to 20 degrees above average \nfor the lower elevations of the mountains. High temperatures will \nrange from the 80s near the coast to 100 to 108 for the Inland \nEmpire with 112 to 116 for the lower deserts. Low temperatures \nwill be mostly in the 70s for the coast, valleys, and high desert \nand in the mid 80s to mid 90s for the lower deserts. Low \ntemperatures will be around 80 for the lower elevations of the \nmountains. The highest HeatRisk is expected on Thursday with \nwidespread Moderate to Extreme HeatRisk for the valleys, inland \ncoastal areas, and lower deserts. While the high temperatures will\nlikely not tie or exceed existing records, many records for \nwarmest overnight low temperatures could be tied or broken, most\nlikely on Thursday and Friday.\n\nA cooling trend is expected for Saturday into the middle of next\nweek as high pressure aloft weakens and shifts to the southeast in\nresponse to a developing trough of low pressure near the \nCalifornia coast which will strengthen lower-level flow onshore. \nHigh temperatures will cool to around average for next Monday with\nlow temperatures cooling to slightly above average. \n\nThere could be some increase in clouds and a chance of\nprecipitation around Tuesday or Wednesday of next week from\nmoisture from a tropical system that has yet to develop.",
